         alt/all 12            : b_required by bin = -1.56, -1.31, -2.06, -2.18, -2.07, -2.21   (median -2.07)

    (c)  L2's shape gate.  The b for which the fitted log-slope p of Delta_req falls to the class ceiling 1/2.
         canonical/stellar 7   : b(p = 1/2) = -6.793   (there A = 5.06, rms 1.865 dex, 45 of 72 rows still positive)
         canonical/all 12      : b(p = 1/2) = -6.062   (there A = 0.22, rms 1.382 dex, 99 of 124 rows still positive)
         Read (c) as an order of magnitude only: at such a b a third of the rows have g_true < g_bar, so the
         power law is fitted to a mutilated cloud (rms 1.4-1.9 dex).  The usable statement is the derivative,
         reported in section 3: dp/db > 0, so no POSITIVE b can ever bring p down to 1/2.
         (b) is identical at both footings by construction -- it equates two directly measured accelerations,
         from which a0 cancels, which is the same invariance L2 records for its cluster/galaxy ratio.

    THE COMPARISON, which is the whole point of the lane:
         required   b = -0.82 (L7, canonical) / -0.68 (L7, alt) / -2.07 (L2 overlap) / -6.79 (L2 slope)
         measured   b in [+0.00, +0.42] for the population, [+0.03, +0.17] for X-COP itself,
                    with a single-cluster empirical floor near -0.20
         The required value is NEGATIVE and the measured value is POSITIVE.  They are not merely different in
         size; they are on opposite sides of zero, so no amount of tightening the measured range can reach it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  5.  IS THE REQUIRED b PHYSICALLY AVAILABLE?  A bias b>0 is non-thermal support: M_true - M_HSE = 
      -(r^2/G rho_g) d(rho_g sigma^2)/dr.  With rho_g ~ r^-alpha_g and sigma roughly constant this is
      sigma^2 = [b/(1-b)] g_HSE r / alpha_g, so b<0 needs sigma^2 < 0 -- not turbulence at all.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

      cluster     alpha_g |  sigma needed at b=+0.20 |  sigma^2 needed at b_req
      A1644          1.29 |                 495 km/s |        -3.34e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)
      A1795          1.80 |                 475 km/s |        -3.55e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)
      A2029          1.79 |                 610 km/s |        -8.01e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)
      A2142          1.62 |                 648 km/s |        -8.70e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)
      A2255          1.35 |                 583 km/s |        -5.84e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)
      A2319          1.45 |                 690 km/s |        -9.96e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)
      A3158          1.74 |                 486 km/s |        -3.90e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)
      A3266          1.26 |                 652 km/s |        -7.67e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)
      A644           1.80 |                 544 km/s |        -6.10e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)
      A85            1.69 |                 534 km/s |        -5.13e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)
      RXC1825        1.56 |                 503 km/s |        -4.35e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)
      ZW1215         1.72 |                 574 km/s |        -7.33e+05 km^2/s^2  (NEGATIVE: impossible)

      a measured b = 0.20 at 0.80 R500 corresponds to sigma_1D = 559 km/s (median), 3.4x Hitomi's
      Perseus measurement of 164 km/s.  Cross-check that this conversion is sane: at a typical kT = 6 keV that
      dispersion is a non-thermal pressure fraction f_nth = rho sigma^2/(rho sigma^2 + P_th) = 25%, against the
      21% Nelson+2014 simulate at this radius -- so b = 0.20 and the simulated turbulence are the same
      statement, and both are a correction that makes the framework's job HARDER.  (sigma is model-dependent:
      constant sigma with rho_g ~ r^-alpha_g; a rising sigma(r) would need less at this radius and more outside.)

      The b the framework needs is NEGATIVE in 12 of 12 clusters, so sigma^2 < 0: it is not a non-thermal
      support at all but its opposite.  Written without turbulence, it is the demand that the measured thermal
      pressure gradient be OVER-stated by a factor 1.82.  This is exactly the branch the lead's own
      CLUSTER_AUDIT.md proves impossible with a non-negative outer boundary pressure ('with zero nonthermal
      pressure at the outer endpoint the required inner nonthermal pressure is negative').

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  6.  CROSS-CHECK.  The lead's audit already stores, per row, `HSE_multiplicative_factor_for_exact_match` --
      the factor F on M_HSE that makes the baryons alone satisfy its EXACT exponential law g_b = g_H(1-e^-g_H/a0).
      That factor IS 1/(1-b) for that law, so b_lead = 1 - 1/F.  Re-deriving it here independently must agree.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

      independent re-solve of every one of 248 stored rows agrees to 2.50e-15 (relative)
      A1795    at 300 kpc: stored F = 0.282  ->  b_lead = -2.540
      A2029    at 300 kpc: stored F = 0.264  ->  b_lead = -2.783
      A2142    at 300 kpc: stored F = 0.307  ->  b_lead = -2.254
      The audit's published table (0.282 / 0.264 / 0.307) therefore already contains this lane's answer, in
      a different notation and for a different kernel: b = -2.55 / -2.79 / -2.26 at 300 kpc.  L18's own
      solve, on the framework's carried kernel and at L7's radius, gives the milder -0.82; both are
      negative and both are far outside anything measured.

